---
Loyalty points: one point per whole pound spent on a domestic order.

This context consumes `shop.orders` only to name its event. It never calls the
checkout, and the checkout doesn't know it exists.
---
context shop.loyalty

consumes shop.orders
consumes bynk { Clock }

--- How many pence earn one point.
const PencePerPoint: Int = 100

--- Each customer's points balance.
agent Points {
	key customer: String

	store balance: Cell[Int] = 0
	---
	The orders already awarded, and the points each earned. A week outlasts
	any redelivery, so an entry can expire once nothing will repeat it.
	---
	store awarded: Cache[String, Int] @ttl(7.days)

	---
	Award an order's points at most once.

	The check and the award are one handler, and an agent commits a handler's
	writes together when it returns, or not at all if it faults. So there is
	no window where the order is marked but the points are missing, or the
	points are in but the order isn't marked. Keeping the mark beside the
	balance is what buys that: a separate dedup store would be a second write
	that can succeed or fail on its own.
	---
	on call award(orderId: String, points: Int) -> Effect[()] given Clock {
		let seen <- awarded.get(orderId)
		match seen {
			Some(_) => (),
			None => {
				do awarded.put(orderId, points)
				let _ <- balance.update((n) => n + points)
			},
		}
	}

	on call current() -> Effect[Int] {
		balance
	}
}

---
Award points for an order. The pattern on the header filters delivery: an
international order still reaches this service, but its generated handler
drops it before this body runs.

Delivery is at least once, so the same order can arrive twice: as a
redelivered emission, or as a second emission when a customer resubmits a
checkout that timed out. Both carry the same `orderId`, so `award` dedups on
that rather than on the envelope's `eventId`, which only catches the first.
---
service AwardPoints from Events(OrderPlaced { region: Region.Domestic, .. }) {
	on event(e: OrderPlaced) -> Effect[()] {
		Points(e.customer).award(e.orderId, e.totalPence / PencePerPoint)
	}
}
